    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d154">

                <pb facs="0404" n="376" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v03/pg376"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">315.3/11–3053: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Mission at the United
                        Nations </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d154-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1953-11-30T18:56:00-05:00">November 30,
                            1953—6:56 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>265. Personal for Lodge from Secretary. I have your 338. Have discussed this
                    matter with British Ambassador. Don’t think they want <pb facs="0405" n="377"
                        xml:id="pg377"/> to be unfriendly but both British and French Govts seem to
                    feel they cannot vote against awards which are sustained by their best legal
                    advice without losing the respect of their own Civil Service, which, as you
                    know, in these European countries is extremely powerful and mutually
                    self-defensive. Their position is that if we have a legal case against the
                    awards, why are we unwilling to present it to the Court? Have done my best here
                    but without result and will have it in mind for Bermuda, if it is still a live
                        issue.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d154-f2"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dulles</hi>
                    </signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d154-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d154-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by the Secretary of State. Documentation on the parliamentary situation
                    at the General Assembly as this matter progressed is not printed; for the most
                    part it is in file 315.3.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d154-f2" n="2" xml:id="d154-f2-content">
                <p>For a summary of developments, see circular instruction CA–3835, Jan. 22, 1954,
                        <hi rendition="#italic">infra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
